ce: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.15048v1\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 1\nL0: Failed to fetch https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.15048v1: Cache miss\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n[2509.14504v1] Introducing OmniGEC: A Silver Multilingual Dataset for Grammatical Error Correction (https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.14504v1)\nciteturn29798view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 9 months ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.14504v1\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 209\nL0: cite0†Skip to main content L1: \nL2:  cite59†Image: Cornell University L3: \nL4:  We gratefully acknowledge support from the Simons Foundation, cite1†member institutions†info.arxiv.org , and all contributors. cite2†Donate†info.arxiv.org L5: \nL6: cite60†Image: arxiv logo > cite3†cs > arXiv:2509.14504v1\nL7: \nL8: cite4†Help†info.arxiv.org | cite5†Advanced Search L9: \nL10:  All fields Title Author Abstract Comments Journal reference ACM classification MSC classification Report number arXiv identifier DOI ORCID arXiv author ID Help pages Full text\nL11: \nL12: Search\nL13: cite61†Image: arXiv logo L14: \nL15: cite6† †www.cornell.edu L16: \nL17:  open search\nL18: \nL19: GO\nL20: \nL21: open navigation menu\nL22: ## quick links\nL23: \nL24:   * cite7†Login L25:   * cite4†Help Pages†info.arxiv.org L26:   * cite8†About†info.arxiv.org L27: \nL28: # Computer Science > Computation and Language\nL29: \nL30: arXiv:2509.14504v1 (cs)\nL31: \nL32: [Submitted on 18 Sep 2025]\nL33: # Title:Introducing OmniGEC: A Silver Multilingual Dataset for Grammatical Error Correction\nL34: \nL35: Authors:cite9†Roman Kovalchuk , cite10†Mariana Romanyshyn , cite11†Petro Ivaniuk L36: \nL37: View a PDF of the paper titled Introducing OmniGEC: A Silver Multilingual Dataset for Grammatical Error Correction, by Roman Kovalchuk and 2 other authors\nL38: \nL39: cite12†View PDF cite13†HTML (experimental) L40: > Abstract:In this paper, we introduce OmniGEC, a collection of multilingual silver-standard datasets for the task of Grammatical Error Correction (GEC), covering eleven languages: Czech, English, Estonian, German, Greek, Icelandic, Italian, Latvian, Slovene, Swedish, and Ukrainian. These datasets facilitate the development of multilingual GEC solutions and help bridge the data gap in adapting English GEC solutions to multilingual GEC.\nL41: The texts in the datasets originate from three sources: Wikipedia edits for the eleven target languages, subreddits from Reddit in the eleven target languages, and the Ukrainian-only UberText 2.0 social media corpus. While Wikipedia edits were derived from human-made corrections, the Reddit and UberText 2.0 data were automatically corrected with the GPT-4o-mini model. The quality of the corrections in the datasets was evaluated both automatically and manually.\nL42: Finally, we fine-tune two open-source large language models - Aya-Expanse (8B) and Gemma-3 (12B) - on the multilingual OmniGEC corpora and achieve state-of-the-art (SOTA) results for paragraph-level multilingual GEC. The dataset collection and the best-performing models are available on Hugging Face.\nL43: Subjects: Computation and Language (cs.CL); Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Machine Learning (cs.LG)\nL44: Cite as: cite14†arXiv:2509.14504 [cs.CL]\nL45:   (or  cite15†arXiv:2509.14504v1 [cs.CL] for this version)\nL46:   cite16†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2509.14504†doi.org L47: \nL48: Focus to learn more\nL49: \nL50:   arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL51: \nL52: Journal reference: Proceedings of the Fourth Ukrainian Natural Language Processing Workshop (UNLP 2025)\nL53: Related DOI:  cite17†https://doi.org/10.18653/v1/2025.unlp-1†doi.org L54: Focus to learn more\nL55: \nL56:   DOI(s) linking to related resources\nL57: ## Submission history\nL58: \nL59: From: Petro Ivaniuk [cite18†view email ]\nL60: [v1] Thu, 18 Sep 2025 00: